---
# An end state (every row old and folded) is not a deadlock; the
# liveness check still sees it.
deadlock_detection: false
---
# Where a session lands in the list and in the sidebar: ordering by last
# activity, what a bookkeeping write may not do to it, the 72 h fold with
# FRESH_MIN, ties, and a queued thread (go/tests/model/README.md).
#
# Code this is checked against:
#   internal/serve/api.go      listSessions: rows sorted (stable) by
#                              Modified, newest first; rowOf: Modified =
#                              the file's mtime, LastAt = lastAt(entries).
#   plugins/history/history.go List: mtime desc, ties broken on id desc
#                              (the stable sort above keeps that).
#   internal/serve/children_api.go  queuedRow: a queued child has no
#                              file; Modified = LastAt = time.Now() on
#                              every read.
#   internal/serve/digest.go   troubled: an unacked failure is trouble
#                              for 7 days (troubleWindow).
#   web/src/status.tsx         sessionSignal/hasFailure: a failure from
#                              the last 72 h needs you, acked or not.
#   web/src/app.tsx            Sidebar: needIds pin a local row under
#                              Needs you and take it out of its group
#                              (listed in server order); byUrgency orders
#                              a group (signal, then lastAt; a tie keeps
#                              the server's order, the sort is stable);
#                              isOld/isTucked fold a row quiet for 72 h
#                              under "N older" unless FRESH_MIN (3)
#                              lifts it back.
#
# The world. One local folder group w holds two sessions the person
# works in, A and B, plus two rows R1 and R2 held running for the whole
# walk (llm-control "block" turns). The running pair is fixed scenery:
# a running row is never old, so it always counts towards FRESH_MIN,
# which is what lets one of A and B fold at all (with only A and B in
# the group, FRESH_MIN keeps both however old). A was created after B,
# so A has the larger id: on a tie the server lists A first. Q is a
# thread of project p, spawned while the child queue is full; it is
# alone in p's group, which always sorts below w (w's running rows give
# it signal 1, a queued or finished Q is 2).
#
# Fields. Observed off the API / the page:
#   last_active  which of A and B the server says was active last: "A",
#                "B", or "tie" (equal lastAt and mtime, to the ms).
#   a, b         "done", "failed" (an unacked failure, trouble) or
#                "seen" (a failure marked seen: still red for 72 h).
#   a_old, b_old the row's lastAt is 72 h or more behind the clock.
#   server       the order of A and B in GET /api/sessions: "AB"/"BA".
#   sidebar      the order of A and B on the page when they list in the
#                same place (both in w, or both under Needs you); "" when
#                one is pinned and the other is in w.
#   a_pinned, a_grouped (b_…)  listed under Needs you / in w's group.
#   a_tucked, b_tucked  folded under w's "N older" line.
#   unfolded     w's "N older" is open (it is remembered while nothing is
#                folded, so it can be true with no line showing).
#   q            Q: "none", "queued" (a pending row, no history yet) or
#                "started" (it wrote its first entry).
#   first        which of A, B, Q the server lists first.
# Tracked by the adapter (it drove the turns, the list cannot tell it):
#   last_turn    whose real turn ended last: "A", "B", or "tie".
#
# Time. ClockPasses72h is one step of 72 h for everything at once: the
# running scenery and a queued Q do not age (running is never old; a
# queued row is re-stamped on every read), A and B do. The 7-day trouble
# window is past the model's horizon: an unacked failure stays pinned.
#
# Where this differs from the flow as first mapped (see the notes of
# the run that wrote it):
# - "group_reordered" is not reachable in this world and is left out:
#   w's running rows give it signal 1 whatever A and B do, so the two
#   groups never swap. Group order is byUrgency's key on groups (min
#   signal, newest lastAt) with the same stable-sort fallback.
# - byUrgency and the group sort have no explicit id tiebreak, but they
#   are not nondeterministic: Array.sort is stable and the rows arrive
#   in the server's order, which breaks ties on id. The spec says what
#   that yields ("AB" on a tie); the invariants hold only as long as the
#   client never re-sorts rows before byUrgency.
# - Needs you lists in the server's order, not byUrgency's.

# Pure helpers (a fizz func cannot be called inside an expression).

def pinned(st, old):
    # hasFailure: trouble (7 d, beyond the model) or a failure < 72 h.
    return st == "failed" or (st == "seen" and not old)

# A before B on a tie: A has the larger id.
def ranked(last):
    if last == "B":
        return "BA"
    return "AB"

role List:
    action Init:
        # B then A each ran one clean turn; A's ended last.
        self.last_turn = "A"
        self.last_active = "A"
        self.a = "done"
        self.b = "done"
        self.a_old = False
        self.b_old = False
        self.unfolded = False
        self.q = "none"
        self.first = "A"
        self.derive()

    # What the list and the page show, from the fields above.
    atomic func derive():
        self.server = ranked(self.last_active)
        self.a_pinned = pinned(self.a, self.a_old)
        self.b_pinned = pinned(self.b, self.b_old)
        self.a_grouped = not self.a_pinned
        self.b_grouped = not self.b_pinned
        # w's group: R1, R2 (running, fresh), then A and B by byUrgency.
        order = ["A", "B"]
        if self.server == "BA":
            order = ["B", "A"]
        grouped = [x for x in order if (x == "A" and self.a_grouped) or (x == "B" and self.b_grouped)]
        olds = [x for x in grouped if (x == "A" and self.a_old) or (x == "B" and self.b_old)]
        fresh = 2 + len(grouped) - len(olds)
        tucked = []
        for x in olds:
            if fresh < 3:
                fresh = fresh + 1
            else:
                tucked.append(x)
        self.a_tucked = "A" in tucked
        self.b_tucked = "B" in tucked
        if self.a_pinned == self.b_pinned:
            # Both in w (fresh rows above the fold), or both pinned
            # (Needs you keeps the server's order).
            self.sidebar = self.server
        else:
            self.sidebar = ""

    # x wrote: it is first in the list, unless a queued row (stamped
    # now on every read) is still above everything.
    atomic func touch(x):
        if self.q != "queued":
            self.first = x

    # A clean turn ends in A / in B.
    atomic action TurnDoneA:
        self.a = "done"
        self.a_old = False
        self.last_turn = "A"
        self.last_active = "A"
        self.touch("A")
        self.derive()

    atomic action TurnDoneB:
        self.b = "done"
        self.b_old = False
        self.last_turn = "B"
        self.last_active = "B"
        self.touch("B")
        self.derive()

    # A turn ends in an error: trouble until acked, red for 72 h after.
    atomic action FailA:
        self.a = "failed"
        self.a_old = False
        self.last_turn = "A"
        self.last_active = "A"
        self.touch("A")
        self.derive()

    atomic action FailB:
        self.b = "failed"
        self.b_old = False
        self.last_turn = "B"
        self.last_active = "B"
        self.touch("B")
        self.derive()

    # The Seen button (POST /ack): clears trouble; the row stays pinned
    # while the failure is under 72 h old (ui_sidebar says the same).
    atomic action AckA:
        require self.a == "failed"
        self.a = "seen"
        self.derive()

    atomic action AckB:
        require self.b == "failed"
        self.b = "seen"
        self.derive()

    # Both finish a clean turn in the same millisecond: equal lastAt and
    # equal mtime. The server breaks the tie on id, the page keeps it.
    atomic action TwoSessionsSameMillisecond:
        self.a = "done"
        self.b = "done"
        self.a_old = False
        self.b_old = False
        self.last_turn = "tie"
        self.last_active = "tie"
        self.touch("A")
        self.derive()

    # The title plugin's "title" / "turn-summary" entries, written after
    # a turn's done or by a summarize backfill of an old session. They
    # are bookkeeping: no field moves.
    atomic action TitleWrittenA:
        self.derive()

    atomic action TitleWrittenB:
        self.derive()

    # 72 h pass with nobody touching A or B.
    atomic fair action ClockPasses72h:
        require not (self.a_old and self.b_old)
        self.a_old = True
        self.b_old = True
        self.derive()

    # w's "N older" line.
    atomic action Unfold:
        require (self.a_tucked or self.b_tucked) and not self.unfolded
        self.unfolded = True

    atomic action Fold:
        require (self.a_tucked or self.b_tucked) and self.unfolded
        self.unfolded = False

    # Something in p spawns Q while the child queue is full.
    atomic action Spawn:
        require self.q == "none"
        self.q = "queued"
        self.first = "Q"

    # A list read while Q waits: its row is re-stamped, nothing moves.
    atomic action QueuedRowPoll:
        require self.q == "queued"
        self.derive()

    # A slot frees; Q writes its first entry, newer than anything else.
    atomic fair action Start:
        require self.q == "queued"
        self.q = "started"
        self.first = "Q"

    # The page's list poll (4 s): a read changes nothing.
    atomic action Poll:
        self.derive()

action Init:
    list = List()

# Bookkeeping never moves a row: what the list says was active last is
# the last real turn.
always assertion BookkeepingDoesNotMove:
    return list.last_active == list.last_turn

# Equal timestamps break on id, on the server and the page alike.
always assertion TieBreaksOnId:
    return list.last_active != "tie" or (list.server == "AB" and list.sidebar in ["AB", ""])

# A local row lists exactly once: pinned under Needs you or in its group.
always assertion ListedOnce:
    return list.a_pinned != list.a_grouped and list.b_pinned != list.b_grouped

# Only a grouped row folds, and FRESH_MIN keeps w at three rows at least:
# with R1 and R2 fresh, at most one of A and B is folded.
always assertion FreshMinHolds:
    return (not list.a_tucked or list.a_grouped) and (not list.b_tucked or list.b_grouped) and not (list.a_tucked and list.b_tucked)

# A folded row is quiet (72 h) and needs nobody.
always assertion OnlyQuietFolds:
    return (not list.a_tucked or (list.a_old and not list.a_pinned)) and (not list.b_tucked or (list.b_old and not list.b_pinned))

# Rows with the same signal list in the same order on the page and in
# the API.
always assertion ServerAndSidebarAgree:
    return list.a_pinned != list.b_pinned or list.sidebar == list.server

# A queued thread is the newest row there is.
always assertion QueuedOnTop:
    return list.q != "queued" or list.first == "Q"

# Liveness: A untouched for 72 h and not needing you does not stay a
# fresh row of w: it folds, or FRESH_MIN keeps it (old, lifted). Holds
# because the clock is fair: turns may keep A fresh, but 72 h keep
# passing between them.
always eventually assertion QuietAFolds:
    return not (list.a_grouped and not list.a_old)

always eventually assertion QuietBFolds:
    return not (list.b_grouped and not list.b_old)

always eventually assertion QueueDrains:
    return list.q != "queued"
